---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="HelpNow+: Enterprise Support That People Could Trust • Case Study"
	breadcrumbs={["VMware", "HelpNow+", "Lead Designer"]}
>
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 1: HERO & TITLE                               -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<!-- Eyebrow -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
			<small class="eyebrow">Case Study 02 // HelpNow+</small>
		</div>

		<!-- Main Title -->
		<h1>Enterprise Support That People Could Trust</h1>

		<!-- Lead Paragraph -->
		<div class="max-w-4xl mb-10">
			<p>
				I led the end-to-end design of <strong>HelpNow+</strong>, a
				service management platform that replaced VMware's legacy IT
				service tool. I designed all three sides of it: where employees
				ask for help, where agents do the work, and where department
				admins define how their services run.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Hero Visual -->
		<figure class="m-0 mb-4">
			<div class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20Home.png"
					alt="HelpNow+ employee home showing search, popular services, knowledge, and recent tickets"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</div>
			<figcaption>
				The employee home: search, services, and ticket status in one
				place.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<!-- Metadata Matrix Box: Role and Timeline Panel -->
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-16">
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">role</small>
				<h4>Lead Designer</h4>
				<p>End-to-end product design, research, and alignment.</p>
				<small class="eyebrow"><time>2022</time></small>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">team</small>
				<h4>Core Squad</h4>
				<p>
					3 Designers (including me), 12+ Developers, 4 Business
					Analysts, 1 PM.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h4>$36M+ Saved</h4>
				<p>
					In the first year. ~40% fewer escalations and 94% positive
					experience.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 2: THE PROBLEM                                -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">problem</small>
				<h2>Every department ran support differently, so people avoided it</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The legacy tool was built for IT. HR, Finance, and
					Facilities were bolted on, each with its own process, and
					requests bounced between teams. Employees gave up and called
					or emailed instead.
				</p>

				<!-- Problem Stats -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ESCALATIONS</small>
						<h4>78 a day</h4>
						<p>On average.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">AVOIDANCE</small>
						<h4>60%</h4>
						<p>Of non-IT users avoided the system entirely.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">PROCESSES</small>
						<h4>Inconsistent</h4>
						<p>Across HR, Finance, IT, and Facilities.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

              <figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/user-problem.png"
								alt="Illustration of requests bouncing between IT, HR, and Workplace teams"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Requests bounced between IT, HR, and Workplace, and
							employees kept calling to ask why.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				<!-- Side by Side: Legacy Before & Cross-team Handoffs -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn5_before.png"
								alt="Legacy ITSM portal before HelpNow+"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Before: built around IT, confusing for everyone else.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/hn3_ecosystem.png" alt="Service ecosystem across IT, HR, Finance, and Facilities" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Service ecosystem</figcaption>
					</figure>
					
				</div>

				
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 3: THE REFRAME                                -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">reframe</small>
				<h2>A better portal wouldn't fix it. The whole system had to change.</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Employees only see the front door. Behind it, agents work
					the queue and admins define every service. A request that
					is confusing to submit is usually confusing to route and
					fulfill too. So I designed for all three sides together.
				</p>
				<p>
					The scope was bigger than the team first realized. VMware
					had no product for service request management, and it was
					the missing piece in VMware's flagship offering. I ran alignment workshops to get the team onto the
					platform scope before we designed screens.
				</p>

				<!-- 3 Sides Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">EMPLOYEES</small>
						<h4>Understand what's happening.</h4>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">AGENTS</small>
						<h4>Understand what needs to happen.</h4>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ADMINS</small>
						<h4>Define how it happens.</h4>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: Operating Model & Workshop -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn-operating-model.png"
								alt="Operating model: admins define services and assign agents, employees browse services, agents resolve the ticket queue, and performance is tracked"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The operating model I designed around: admins define,
							employees request, agents resolve.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn-scope-workshop.png"
								alt="Whiteboard covered in sticky notes from the scope alignment workshop"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Aligning the team on the platform scope.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>

				<!-- Supporting Screens -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/hn-roles.png" alt="Actor map of the employee, agent, and admin connected through one service" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Actor map: how employees, agents, and admins connect through one service</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/hn-scope-framing.png" alt="Framing the platform opportunity: user groups, journeys, and key features" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Scope framing</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 4: DECISION 01 - EMPLOYEES                    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">employees</small>
				<h2>Search alone failed. The catalog came back.</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Our first design stripped the home down to one search box,
					backed by NLP. It tested badly. People asked where the
					catalog was and whether the system was broken. You can't
					search for a service you don't know exists.
				</p>
				<p>
					The final home shows the catalog and popular services for
					people browsing, and keeps search, pins, and recent tickets
					for people who know what they want.
				</p>

				<!-- Full-Width Images: stacked, spaced by the grid gap -->
				<div class="grid gap-6 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/hn2_heuristic.png" alt="Heuristic review of the legacy portal" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Heuristic review</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/failure.png"
								alt="The search-first concept and what people said in testing"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							First try: search only. "Where's the catalog?"
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn4_final_employee.png"
								alt="Final HelpNow+ employee home"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Final: a visible catalog, with search as the fast
							path.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>

				
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 5: DECISION 02 - TRUST                        -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">employees</small>
				<h2>Privacy and progress, visible on every ticket</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The old tool had no privacy segmentation, so sensitive HR
					requests went by phone or email. And once a ticket was
					submitted, people couldn't tell what was happening, so they
					escalated.
				</p>
				<p>
					In HelpNow+, tickets can be marked <strong>Private</strong>,
					backed by role-based permissions. Every ticket shows its
					stages, expected completion, and an <strong>Escalate</strong
					> action, so people know what's happening and have a next
					step besides calling.
				</p>

				<figure class="m-0 pt-2">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20Ticket%20Detail%20%28Simple%29.png"
							alt="Employee view of an onboarding ticket with progress stages and updates"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						The employee sees each stage, who's working on it, and
						when it will be done.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Supporting Screens -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20My%20Tickets.png" alt="Employee ticket list with status" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>My Tickets</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Permissions.png" alt="Role-based permissions for private tickets" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Role-based permissions</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 6: DECISION 03 - AGENTS                       -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">agents</small>
				<h2>Agents see what to work on next, not just what's open</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Agents were working from a legacy ticketing tool that
					treated every ticket the same. But one request can hold
					several kinds of work, like new hardware, software, and an
					access badge, each with its own owner.
				</p>
				<p>
					I designed an opinionated queue that sorts tasks by SLA
					risk: breached, at risk, and on track. Requests break into
					items and tasks, so each team works only its part. Team
					leads get a view of workload by agent to balance the load.
				</p>

				<div class="grid gap-5 pt-2">
                  <figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/hn-fulfiller-ticket-types.png" alt="Multiple ticket types from text model to sketch to wireframe" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Multiple ticket types</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fullfiller%20-%20My%20Tasks.png"
								alt="Agent task queue grouped by SLA status"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The queue, grouped by SLA risk.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20Ticket%20Detail%20%28Onboarding%29.png"
								alt="Agent view of an onboarding request split into tasks across HR, IT, and Facilities"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							One request, split into tasks for HR, IT, and
							Facilities.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20Dashboard.png" alt="Agent dashboard" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Agent dashboard</figcaption>
					</figure>
					
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 7: DECISION 04 - ADMINS                       -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">admins</small>
				<h2>One platform, each department's own process</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Forcing every department into IT's process was why the old
					tool failed. Instead, I mapped each department's statuses to
					a shared set of stages. HR, IT, and Facilities keep their
					own language, and the platform still tracks every request the
					same way.
				</p>
				<p>
					Admins also build their own services in four guided steps:
					details, intake form, workflow, and review. Employee
					onboarding, for example, runs 11 steps across HR, IT, and
					Facilities.
				</p>

				<div class="grid gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn-admin-status-process.png"
								alt="Status to stage mapping and sketches of department-specific status customization"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Mapping each department's statuses to shared stages.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Create%20Service%20%283.%20Workflow%29.png"
								alt="Service builder workflow with parallel branches across HR, IT, and Facilities"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The onboarding workflow, built by an HR admin.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>

				<!-- Supporting Screens -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Create%20Service%20%281.%20Service%20Details%29.png" alt="Service builder step 1: details" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Builder: details</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Create%20Service%20%282.%20Intake%20Form%29.png" alt="Service builder step 2: intake form" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Builder: intake form</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/hn-admin-review-publish.png" alt="Service builder step 4: review and publish" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Builder: review</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Ticket%20Status%20Customization.png" alt="Department status customization" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Status customization</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20Service%20Health.png" alt="Service health dashboard" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Service health</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Edit%20SLA.png" alt="SLA policy editor" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>SLA policies</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Rules%20Automation.png" alt="Routing and automation rules" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Routing rules</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Departments.png" alt="Department settings" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Departments</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Service%20Guides.png" alt="Service guides" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Service guides</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Branding.png" alt="Department branding" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Branding</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 8: OUTCOME & RESULTS                          -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article id="outcome" class="case-section border-b-0 pb-0">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h2>Results</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<!-- Impact Scorecard Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-5 mb-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">Savings</small>
						<h4>$36M+ Saved</h4>
						<p>
							In licensing and phone-support costs in the first
							year (2022).
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">Escalations</small>
						<h4>~40% Drop</h4>
						<p>In quarterly average escalations.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">Experience</small>
						<h4>94% Positive</h4>
						<p>Of users reported a positive experience.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<p>
					I validated the agent and admin experiences with 13
					moderated usability sessions: 9 agents and 4 department
					admins across regions.
				</p>

				<!-- Supporting Screens -->
				<div class="grid pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img src="/images-helpnow/hn-research.png" alt="Research participants, methods, and artifacts" />
						</div>
						<figcaption>Research: 13 moderated sessions</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>

				<!-- Closing Reflection -->
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						"Support isn't one screen. It's one service, seen three
						different ways."
					</p>
				</blockquote>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- Page Footer Navigation -->
	<footer
		class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-black/15 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4"
	>
		<a href="/" class="btn-secondary flex items-center gap-2">
			<span>←</span>
			<span>Back to homepage</span>
		</a>
		<a
			href="#top"
			onclick="event.preventDefault(); window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' });"
			class="btn-secondary flex items-center gap-1.5 cursor-pointer"
			title="Scroll to top"
		>
			<span>Top</span>
			<span>↑</span>
		</a>
		<a
			href="/case-study/devhub"
			class="btn-primary flex items-center gap-2"
			title="Next Case Study: DevHub"
		>
			<span>Next case study</span>
			<span>→</span>
		</a>
	</footer>
</CaseStudyLayout>
